## Proof

**Yes, it is possible.**

**Construction.** Define a norm $\|\cdot\|$ on $\mathbb{R}^2$ whose closed unit ball $B$ is the convex hull of the six points
$$\pm v_1,\; \pm v_2,\; \pm v_3, \qquad v_1=(2,0),\; v_2=(1,2),\; v_3=(-1,3).$$
Since $B$ is a centrally symmetric convex body with nonempty interior, it is the unit ball of a unique norm.

**$B$ is a strictly convex hexagon.** The six vertices, ordered counterclockwise, are
$$v_1=(2,0),\; v_2=(1,2),\; v_3=(-1,3),\; -v_1=(-2,0),\; -v_2=(-1,-2),\; -v_3=(1,-3).$$
The consecutive edge vectors are $e_1=(-1,2)$, $e_2=(-2,1)$, $e_3=(-1,-3)$, and $e_4=-e_1$, $e_5=-e_2$, $e_6=-e_3$. The cross products of consecutive edges are
$$e_1\times e_2 = 3>0,\quad e_2\times e_3 = 7>0,\quad e_1\times e_3 = 5>0,$$
and the remaining three repeat by central symmetry. All are positive, so $B$ is a strictly convex hexagon.

**Key linear relation.** Since $v_1=2e_1$ and $v_2=e_1+2e_2$, we get $e_1=v_1/2$ and $e_2=v_2/2-v_1/4$. Then
$$v_3=(-1,3)=-e_1+3e_2=-\tfrac{5}{4}v_1+\tfrac{3}{2}v_2. \tag{$\star$}$$

**The isometry group is $\{\pm\mathrm{id}\}$.** Any linear isometry $T$ satisfies $T(B)=B$. Since $T$ is a linear isomorphism, it permutes the extreme points (vertices) and preserves adjacency, so $T$ acts as an automorphism of the 6-cycle $v_1\to v_2\to v_3\to -v_1\to -v_2\to -v_3\to v_1$. Using 0-based indexing ($v_0=v_1,\ldots,v_5=-v_3$), the automorphisms are rotations $\sigma_k(i)=i+k$ and reflections $\sigma_k(i)=-i+k$ (indices mod 6).

*Rotations.* If $T(v_i)=v_{i+k}$, then by linearity and $(\star)$:
$$v_{k+2}=T(v_3)=-\tfrac{5}{4}T(v_1)+\tfrac{3}{2}T(v_2)=-\tfrac{5}{4}v_k+\tfrac{3}{2}v_{k+1}.$$
Checking each $k$:
- $k=0$: $-\frac54(2,0)+\frac32(1,2)=(-1,3)=v_2$. ✓
- $k=1$: $-\frac54(1,2)+\frac32(-1,3)=(-\frac{11}{4},2)\neq(-2,0)$. ✗
- $k=2$: $(-\frac74,-\frac{15}{4})\neq(-1,-2)$. ✗
- $k=3$: $-\frac54(-2,0)+\frac32(-1,-2)=(1,-3)=v_5$. ✓
- $k=4$: $(\frac{11}{4},-2)\neq(2,0)$. ✗
- $k=5$: $(\frac74,\frac{15}{4})\neq(1,2)$. ✗

Only $k=0$ (the identity) and $k=3$ (which gives $T(v_1)=-v_1$, $T(v_2)=-v_2$, i.e. $T=-\mathrm{id}$) are compatible.

*Reflections.* If $T(v_i)=v_{-i+k}$, then $v_{k-2}=-\frac54 v_k+\frac32 v_{k-1}$. Checking each $k$:
- $k=0$: $(-1,-\frac92)\neq(-1,-2)$. ✗
- $k=1$: $(\frac74,-\frac52)\neq(1,-3)$. ✗
- $k=2$: $(\frac{11}{4},-\frac34)\neq(2,0)$. ✗
- $k=3$: $(1,\frac92)\neq(1,2)$. ✗
- $k=4$: $(-\frac74,\frac52)\neq(-1,3)$. ✗
- $k=5$: $(-\frac{11}{4},\frac34)\neq(-2,0)$. ✗

No reflection is compatible with linearity.

**Conclusion.** The only linear maps preserving $B$ are $\mathrm{id}$ and $-\mathrm{id}$. Hence the norm $\|\cdot\|$ with unit ball $B=\operatorname{conv}\{\pm(2,0),\pm(1,2),\pm(-1,3)\}$ has linear isometry group exactly $\{\pm\mathrm{id}\}$.

